{
  "name": "Monthly HPL Assignment, Termination, Expiration Report",
  "change": "Generate the report automatically from Global Contracts on a schedule and send it to a self-service subscription list instead of Contract Administration compiling and sending it by hand",
  "reason": "The mail never shows the report being compiled by hand. The only clue (1360.eml) is that Contract Administration already pulls such lists straight from Global Contracts, so the 45 minutes per issue and the 55 minutes a month saved are unsupported, and what is left (running a pull, attaching it, sending it to a saved list) takes only a few minutes.",
  "research": "Find mail showing how the report is actually built (for example, retyping or checking contract entries by hand, or a revision caused by compiling errors, such as the February 2001 revision mentioned in 2114.eml) or naming the time it takes; without that, the savings stay at a few minutes a month."
}

{
  "name": "Manual POPS overrides when MIPS feeds zeros for incomplete meters",
  "change": "Have the nightly MIPS-to-POPS feed mark incomplete meter volumes as incomplete instead of zero, so POPS keeps the Gas Control estimate.",
  "reason": "The fix was already being built when the problem surfaced (935.eml: the programmers were working on it that day), and the mail shows only one incident clearly caused by the zero feed, so the 2-per-month rate and the ongoing saving are not supported.",
  "research": "Look for mail after March 2000 showing zeroed meters that MIPS still sent after the vendor fix (meters named, override requests to Volume Management), or a list of the affected meters (Tom Acton asked George Grant for one in 935.eml), to show the work continued and how often."
}

{
  "name": "Double scheduling and allocation of PG&E Texas (PGEV) transport under ENA plus an ENA/HPL desk-to-desk deal",
  "change": "Assign ENA's PGEV transport contracts to HPL so the gas is nominated, held and allocated under one contract with no desk-to-desk deal.",
  "reason": "The person who asked for this, Daren Farmer, decided against it a year later. In April 2001 (155.eml) he wrote that on PGEV 'we have been transporting the gas under ENA' and 'We want to keep these transport contracts with ENA', because HPL was being split off for AEP. The other cited mail is about different problems, and the daily and monthly minutes are guesses.",
  "research": "Only new evidence would change this: mail after April 2001 showing that management decided to move the PGEV transport to HPL after all, or that the ENA leg and the desk-to-desk leg still had to be nominated and allocated by hand once HPL went to AEP."
}

{
  "name": "Deal documents for Arthur Andersen's monthly originations testing",
  "change": "File each originated deal's TAGG ticket, executed confirmation and forwards detail in a read-only, deal-number folder that the auditors open themselves.",
  "reason": "The mail shows only two single-deal requests to Giron (Jan and Mar 2001) across about eight months of AA monthly-testing mail, not one a month. The 3-week open request was caused by Giron missing it while on vacation (118.eml), not by the process. So the saving is about 35 minutes every three or four months, far too small for a filing system that covers every originated deal.",
  "research": "Find originations deal-document requests from AA (Mark Austin, Sharon Smith) to other desk leads or in other months, for example in Keiser's, Baumbach's or Love's mailboxes, to show the requests really come monthly or across several desks, and find any mail showing how long putting a deal packet together takes."
}

{
  "name": "Month-end basis and index roll-off double calc",
  "today": "Each month at the basis and index roll-off, every gas book administrator has to calculate and officialize their book twice under two post IDs: a 100% basis calc for the TDS download and reports, then a 50% roll-off calc for the V@R benchmark. The order is fixed: calc 100%, officialize, load to TDS, unofficialize, calc 50%, officialize. This repeats on the roll-off nights until indexes are set, and the procedure is complex enough that desk leads planned classroom training on it.",
  "change": "Have the risk system run both roll-off calcs per book in one scheduled batch, so book administrators calc once instead of toggling official status by hand.",
  "explanation": "The risk system team adds a roll-off batch job. For each book it produces the 100% prompt-basis calc under a TDS post ID and loads it to TDS, and the 50%/50% roll-off calc under the official V@R post ID. The risk lead (today Jeff Gossett) sets the roll-off and roll-in percentages once per month as parameters. On roll-off nights each book administrator reviews the two results and runs reports off the 100% calc. They no longer recalculate, officialize, unofficialize and re-officialize by hand, and the risk of feeding V@R from the wrong post ID goes away.",
  "occurrences": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/631.eml",
      "quote": "Again this month, you are going to have to calculate your basis positions \n(officialize) twice."
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/631.eml",
      "quote": "Just to be clear,  calc your 100% basis calc first.  Officialize.  Load your \npositions into TDS. Unofficialize. Calc your 50% basis calc.  Officialize.",
      "note": "The manual sequence each book administrator runs: a second full calc plus the officialize, unofficialize and re-officialize steps that the batch would remove."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/631.eml",
      "quote": "For the V@R calculations we will need to roll 50% of basis off \ntonight,  and 50% tommorrow.",
      "note": "The roll-off covers at least two nights, so the double calc is done on each of them."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/631.eml",
      "quote": "We will need two calcs until indexes are set.",
      "note": "The double calc continues every night until indexes are set, so two nights a month is a low estimate."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/631.eml",
      "quote": "To: Jennifer Bagwell/NA/Enron@ENRON, Anne Bike/Corp/Enron@Enron, Chris \nWalker/HOU/ECT@ECT, Jason Wolfe/NA/Enron@ENRON, Dawn C Kenne/HOU/ECT@ECT, \nDarron C Giron/HOU/ECT@ECT",
      "note": "The instruction goes to the people who run the books. Five of them are counted, plus Kevin Radous, to whom Giron forwarded it. Not counted: Giron, and the book administrators under the five other desk leads on the first distribution."
    },
    {
      "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/477.eml",
      "quote": "Topics should include:  NX3 roll-off, the curveshift associate with basis NX3 \nroll-off and where it will show up on the p&l, what days we will roll in \nindex - the percentages",
      "note": "The roll-off and its percentages are a manual procedure that staff must be trained on, which supports setting the percentages centrally."
    }
  ],
  "stalls": [],
  "ongoing": true,
  "stated_rate": {
    "count": 1,
    "per": "month",
    "file": "corpus/giron-d/sent/631.eml",
    "quote": "Again this month"
  },
  "minutes": 180
}

{
  "name": "ENA-to-Burlington capacity release upside sharing",
  "change": "Have Transwestern billing (CBS) compute ENA's release credit and TW's 50% marketing fee automatically each month instead of a hand calculation and manual invoice adjustment.",
  "reason": "The mail shows one special deal on one contract (24924) handled twice, for January and February 2002, with no sign it continued; the time spent was mostly on whether to pay ENA at all while ENA owed TW money, which billing automation would not remove; and a CBS change for one contract would cost more than the roughly 45 minutes a month it could save.",
